  "answer_text": "## Why Does `new_list = my_list` Affect the Original?\n\n### The Root Cause: References, Not Copies\n\nAssignment statements in Python do **not** copy objects \u2014 they create **bindings** between a target and an object. In other words, when you use the assignment operator `=` to copy a list by assigning an existing list variable to a new list variable, you're not actually creating a copy of the list; you're just creating an **alias** that points to the **exact same location in memory** where the original list object exists.\n\nWhen you assign one variable to another, both variables are referencing the **same object** in memory, not two separate ones. This means both variables point to the same object via their references. When more than one variable references the same object, it's called a **shared reference**. Any modification to a shared mutable object via one of the variables that points to it **affects the other variable** that references the same object.\n\n---\n\n### How to Properly Copy a List\n\nGenerally speaking, there are two ways of making an independent copy of a list: a **shallow copy** and a **deep copy**.\n\n---\n\n#### \u2705 Method 1: `list.copy()` \u2014 Shallow Copy\nThe `copy()` method is a built-in method in Python that creates a shallow copy of a list. This method is simple and highly efficient for cloning a list.\n\n```python\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nnew_list = my_list.copy()\n```\n\n---\n\n#### \u2705 Method 2: Slice Operator `[:]` \u2014 Shallow Copy\nThis method creates a new list by slicing all elements from the original. It's concise and performs well. The `[:]` operator selects all elements and creates a new list, independent of the original.\n\n```python\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nnew_list = my_list[:]\n```\n\n---\n\n#### \u2705 Method 3: `list()` Constructor \u2014 Shallow Copy\nThis method uses Python's `list()` constructor to generate a copy of the original list.\n\n```python\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nnew_list = list(my_list)\n```\n\n---\n\n#### \u2705 Method 4: `copy.deepcopy()` \u2014 Deep Copy (for Nested Lists)\n\nThe three methods above are all **shallow copies**, which is fine for flat (non-nested) lists. However, although making a shallow copy of a list produces a true copy of the original list, any modifications to the **nested elements** within it will be reflected in both lists. The reason is that the nested list in the copied list uses the same shared reference as the one in the original list. This is why it's called a *shallow* copy \u2014 because only a new top-level object is created while anything deeper uses a shared reference with the original list.\n\nFor nested lists, you should use `copy.deepcopy()`:\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\nmy_list = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nnew_list = copy.deepcopy(my_list)\n```\n\nThe copied elements in a deep copy have their own pointers and are **not** referenced to the original elements. Therefore, no matter how you modify the deep copy, the changes will **not** be reflected on the original list.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Method | Type | Safe for Flat Lists | Safe for Nested Lists |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `new_list = my_list` | Reference | \u274c | \u274c |\n| `my_list.copy()` | Shallow | \u2705 | \u274c |\n| `my_list[:]` | Shallow | \u2705 | \u274c |\n| `list(my_list)` | Shallow | \u2705 | \u274c |\n| `copy.deepcopy(my_list)` | Deep | \u2705 | \u2705 |",
